  "dom_id": "step-q316704",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981281/assets/ce15895aa3a0f7e7cbea820e06a3c56aadef1d440b59120db17c5df394970adb.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a cubic polynomial $f(x).$\nFind $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the cubic term is $-7.$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{3}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$x\\,+$\n{{field-4}}",
  "worked_solution": "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=1$ and ${x}_{2,3}=\\pm 4\\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=-7.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-7(x-1)(x-4\\text{i})(x-(-4\\text{i})) \\\\  & =-7(x-1)(x-4\\text{i})(x+4\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7(x-1)(x-4\\text{i})(x+4\\text{i}) & = \\\\ -7(x-1)({x}^{2}+x(4\\text{i})-x(4\\text{i})-(4\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ -7(x-1)({x}^{2}+16) & = \\\\ -7({x}^{3}+16x-{x}^{2}-16) & = \\\\ -7{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-112x+112.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-7{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-112x+112.$",
